  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 144 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Temperatures will be hottest Thursday and Friday, \nwith heat indices peaking around 100 to 102 degrees Friday.\n\nHigh pressure aloft will be centered over the SE states Thursday, \nshifting slowly off the SE Atlantic seaboard Friday. Dew points are \nforecast to rise from the mid 60s Thursday into the lower 70s on \nFriday as a front approaches from the north. Actual highs will reach \nthe lower to mid 90s on Thursday and Friday, but higher moisture \nlevels Friday will mean heat indices will likely peak then. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The chance of mainly PM thunderstorms will go up \nFriday, peak Saturday, and lower again by Sunday.  \n\nA weak mid level trough is forecast to develop over the Ohio Valley \nregion Friday and Saturday then dampen as it moves toward the \ncentral Appalachians this weekend. The approach of the weak cold \nfront Friday should increase the chance of a few late day storms. \nHowever, the best chance will come Saturday as the dampening trough \nand the weakening cold front will be nearby. The risk of severe \nstorms looks low even with the moderately high instability forecast. \nShear will be lacking. Highs will be in the mid 80s to lower 90s NW \nto S Saturday. The chance of storms will be on the wane again by \nSunday with rising heights and the front washing out. Highs will \nagain rise to 90 or slightly higher for most of the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Trending hotter and drier with strong upper level \nhigh pressure expected Monday and Tuesday. \n\nUpper heights are expected to begin rising again Sunday over the SE \nstates, peaking Tuesday over the Tennessee Valley or Alabama \nvicinity at 597 dm. This will mean high heat and humidity will \nreturn Sunday through at least Tuesday. Highs will return well into \nthe lower to mid 90s both Monday and Tuesday, with heat indices \nprojected at or around 100 in the east. POP will be less than 15 \npercent.",
